WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.390 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  what did you first think when you walked  into this facility i thought it was a  mars it's incredible yeah it looks like  it looks like a spaceship  you don't realize the magnitude of  something like this a project like this  until you see it with your own ice  this is the largest vertical farm in  canada it uses the latest agricultural  technology to grow fresh produce inside  all year long  cesar cappa is the head grower at  goodleaf farms  at this 45 000 square foot facility in  guelph ontario he grows baby lettuce  spinach arugula and an assortment of  microgreens  led lights replace the sun and the  plants receive nutrients through water  rather than soil  every aspect of this environment is  controlled  from the hue of the lights to the amount  of carbon dioxide in the air  the result is astounding higher crop  yields that require less space and 95  percent less water  and there's no need for pesticides  working on a farm is nothing new to you  but i imagine that this is not exactly  the farm you grew up on yeah exactly  yeah i grew up in a small farm in  argentina this is completely different  but the objective is the same is  sustainability is food security  canada imports most of its leafy greens  from california  but recent supply chain disruptions have  exposed how precarious it is to rely on  produce from abroad we had labor  disruptions but we also had the problem  that california is in the middle of an  unprecedented drought and even when the  system is working perfectly  quality is really low even in the major  cities  lenoir newman is the director of the  food and agriculture institute at the  university of the fraser valley  by the time we get lettuce in british  columbia in vancouver it's already  traveled for eight days and its shelf  life is really only about 10 days when  it comes into the distributor they  usually throw away a third to 40 percent  of what they bought and when you take  what's left home you've only got a  couple of days to make a salad max  [Music]  this is exactly what barry murchie hopes  vertical farming will solve he's the ceo  of goodleaf farms and wants to rid our  lives of empty shelves and mushy leaves  so you take a look at all of those  elements and you say is there an  alternative is there a better way and  this is an example of how technology and  agriculture has merged to create an  alternative that is really better in  every element and is this something that  you can taste absolutely it's really  like going to your garden any day of the  year and picking something fresh because  it tastes like it's fresh at your garden  there are now hundreds of vertical farms  across asia europe and north america  where real estate is scarce and there  are a lot of mouths to feed  vertical farms could also help remedy  food insecurity in rural and remote  parts of canada  these are technologies that could get  more food  into regions where food is very  expensive  and often just plain unavailable  the technology is relatively new  leafy greens and small fruits thrive in  this type of system but other produce  still poses a challenge  and while traditional farms are  vulnerable to climate change  vertical farms are at the mercy of  energy prices  the lights require a lot of electricity  how sustainable these farms are depends  in part on where that power comes from  good leaf estimates its carbon footprint  is half that of a traditional farm  our challenge now is we just need to  build more of these  fruits and vegetables make up only part  of the average canadian's diet we also  love to eat meat  and despite the popularity of  plant-based alternatives the world's  appetite for animal protein is climbing  and it brings with it some big  challenges the meat industry is one of  the largest contributors to greenhouse  gas emissions it is one of the world's  largest land users this is on top of the  fact that there are serious ethical  concerns with animal protein  we've really focused this last 50 years  on making protein cheap  and we've done that at the expense of  animal welfare  the magnitude of this dilemma has given  rise to a new industry  cellular agriculture  where scientists grow animal products  without the animals  at the forefront of this protein  revolution is wild type  all right here's our small piece of wild  type salmon this sushi grade salmon  fillet was grown entirely in a lab  wild-type co-founder arya elfinbine got  the idea while visiting a ranch  as he watched cattle graze where a lush  rainforest once was he got to thinking  do we need animals to have meat could we  just create what we consume just outside  of the animal that's kind of where where  it began  when it came time to decide which  protein to focus on arya says salmon was  an early front-runner  salmon is the most consumed fin fish in  the u.s it's also one of the most  versatile fish that we consume in terms  of all the different culinary traditions  for ben friedman the head of product at  wild type  the choice was more personal  i grew up in the pacific northwest from  a family that spent a lot of time salmon  fishing  and just over the course of my own short  lifetime i've seen the population  decline in in a really incredible way  and so for me i think of wild type as an  opportunity to take pressure off the  wild fisheries  there are other benefits too  wild-type salmon is free of mercury  microplastics and other contaminants  commonly found in seafood and it only  takes four to six weeks to grow a fillet  like this one  in comparison farm salmon takes about  three years to go from egg to harvest  lab-grown protein or cultured meat is an  emerging technology  toronto startup celaegtech gave us a  closer look at how these cells are grown  co-founder valentin fulga and his team  are working on a replacement for snapper  so what's in this little container  you're holding what we have here is the  cells  so  they are growing all the time and you  can see here for example this cell is  dividing  the living cells are set in a  nutrient-rich liquid where they grow  until they run out of space you see here  all the surfaces full of cells it means  that we can move them they need the new  home they need a bigger home  scaling up this process is the biggest  hurdle for that  cells need to be grown in bioreactors  large tanks like you'd see at a brewery  it's a complex and costly process  but wild-type and other cellular  agriculture companies are forging ahead  as they await regulatory approval  like all new technology wild-type salmon  will come at a premium at first  our mission is one of greater  accessibility that's not one that we can  reach if we are just selling expensive  salmon  once the price of cellular agriculture  products drops below  animal produced protein cost then things  get interesting we're going to see large  producers they're saying well my  customers need hamburgers i can  buy this product it's cheaper it's  healthier it's better for the  environment cultured meat is not  destined to replace the trillion dollar  meat industry at least not anytime soon  but it will be an ethical and  sustainable alternative for those who  don't want to give up meat  what we eat changes 150 years ago we ate  a lot of wild game i quite confidently  do predict that in a hundred years  there'll be  very little animal agriculture on earth  we'll look back at industrial slaughter  and we'll say wow it's amazing we did  that  and wow we have a way better product

